    {"id": 7, "compendiumNo": 157, "subCategory": "Constitution & Preamble", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "In the South African Constitution-making process highlighted in NCERT Class IX Chapter 2, who became the first President of democratic South Africa in 1994 after spending 28 years in Robben Island prison?", "options": {"a": "Nelson Mandela", "b": "Desmond Tutu", "c": "F.W. de Klerk", "d": "Oliver Tambo"}, "correctOption": "a", "correctedSentence": "Nelson Mandela", "rule": "Imprisoned for 28 years on Robben Island, Nelson Mandela became South Africa's first democratic President on 26 April 1994 ('The Long Walk to Freedom').", "distractor": "Option (c) — F.W. de Klerk was the outgoing white President.", "shortcut": "28 years Robben Island + 26 April 1994 = Nelson Mandela."},
    {"id": 8, "compendiumNo": 158, "subCategory": "Constitution & Preamble", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Which of the following is NOT a feature of a 'Secular State' as explained in NCERT Class VIII (Social and Political Life-III)?", "options": {"a": "One religious community does not dominate another", "b": "Some members do not dominate other members of the same religious community", "c": "The State enforces a particular official religion to maintain national unity", "d": "The State neither enforces any particular religion nor takes away religious freedom"}, "correctOption": "c", "correctedSentence": "The State enforces a particular official religion to maintain national unity", "rule": "India has no official state religion and prevents both inter-religious and intra-religious domination through distancing and principled intervention (e.g., banning untouchability).", "distractor": "Option (b) — Intra-religious domination is explicitly covered in NCERT Class VIII.", "shortcut": "Indian Secularism = No official state religion + principled intervention."},
    {"id": 9, "compendiumNo": 159, "subCategory": "Constitution & Preamble", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Why is the Constitution of India referred to as a 'Living Document' in NCERT Class XI Chapter 9?", "options": {"a": "Because it cannot be amended under any circumstances", "b": "Because it balances flexibility to adapt to changing societal needs with rigidity to protect its basic structure", "c": "Because it is rewritten every ten years by Parliament", "d": "Because it applies only to citizens born after 1950"}, "correctOption": "b", "correctedSentence": "Balances flexibility to adapt with rigidity to protect basic structure", "rule": "Through Article 368, the Constitution adapts to changing social needs while safeguarding its Basic Structure.", "distractor": "Option (a) — An unamendable constitution becomes static, not a living document.", "shortcut": "Living Document = Balance of Flexibility + Rigidity (Article 368)."},
    {"id": 10, "compendiumNo": 160, "subCategory": "Constitution & Preamble", "type": "Spot the Error", "prompt": "In the landmark Kesavananda Bharati case (1973) discussed in NCERT Class XI Chapter 9, what crucial constitutional doctrine did the Supreme Court establish? (a) Parliament can amend any part of the Constitution, including abolishing Fundamental Rights completely | (b) Parliament can amend any part of the Constitution so long as it does not alter or destroy its 'Basic Structure' | (c) Directive Principles are completely subordinate to ordinary laws | (d) The President can dissolve the Rajya Sabha during an emergency", "options": {"a": "(a) In the landmark Kesavananda Bharati case (1973) discussed in NCERT Class XI Chapter 9, what crucial constitutional doctrine did the Supreme Court establish?", "b": "(b) Parliament can amend any part of the Constitution, including abolishing Fundamental Rights completely |", "c": "(c) Parliament can amend any part of the Constitution so long as it does not alter or destroy its 'Basic Structure' |", "d": "(d) Directive Principles are completely subordinate to ordinary laws |"}, "correctOption": "b", "correctedSentence": "Parliament cannot alter or destroy its 'Basic Structure'", "rule": "The 1973 Kesavananda Bharati judgment ruled that Parliament's amending power under Article 368 cannot violate the 'Basic Structure' of the Constitution.", "distractor": "Option (a) — Reflects the government's pre-1973 stance, which the Court rejected.", "shortcut": "Kesavananda Bharati (1973) = Basic Structure Doctrine. Chapter 2: Fundamental Rights, DPSP & Fundamental Duties — NCERT Class IX & XI (Q161–Q170)"},
    {"id": 11, "compendiumNo": 161, "subCategory": "FR, DPSP & Duties", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Which Fundamental Right was deleted from Part III by the 44th Amendment Act, 1978 and made a legal right under Article 300A (NCERT Class XI Chapter 2)?", "options": {"a": "Right to Constitutional Remedies", "b": "Right to Property", "c": "Right to Education", "d": "Right against Exploitation"}, "correctOption": "b", "correctedSentence": "Right to Property", "rule": "The 44th Amendment (1978) deleted the Right to Property (earlier Art 31) from Fundamental Rights and made it a legal right under Article 300A, leaving six Fundamental Rights.", "distractor": "Option (c) — Right to Education (Art 21A) was added by the 86th Amendment (2002).", "shortcut": "44th Amendment (1978) → Removed Right to Property → Legal right under Art 300A."},
    {"id": 12, "compendiumNo": 162, "subCategory": "FR, DPSP & Duties", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Which Fundamental Right was called the 'heart and soul of the Constitution' by Dr. B.R. Ambedkar (NCERT Class XI Chapter 2)?", "options": {"a": "Right to Equality (Articles 14–18)", "b": "Right to Freedom (Articles 19–22)", "c": "Right to Constitutional Remedies (Article 32)", "d": "Cultural and Educational Rights (Articles 29–30)"}, "correctOption": "c", "correctedSentence": "Right to Constitutional Remedies (Article 32)", "rule": "Dr. Ambedkar called Article 32 the 'heart and soul of the Constitution' because it allows citizens to move the Supreme Court for enforcement of Fundamental Rights.", "distractor": "Option (a) — Ambedkar specifically conferred the title on Article 32.", "shortcut": "\"Heart and soul of Constitution\" (Ambedkar) = Article 32."},
    {"id": 13, "compendiumNo": 163, "subCategory": "FR, DPSP & Duties", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Which Constitutional Writ is issued by a higher court when a lower court has considered a case going beyond its jurisdiction (NCERT Class XI Chapter 2)?", "options": {"a": "Habeas Corpus", "b": "Mandamus", "c": "Prohibition", "d": "Quo Warranto"}, "correctOption": "c", "correctedSentence": "Prohibition", "rule": "Habeas Corpus (produce arrested person), Mandamus (command public official), Prohibition (stop lower court exceeding jurisdiction), Quo Warranto (challenge unauthorized office holding), Certiorari (transfer case to higher court).", "distractor": "Option (b) — Mandamus commands a public official to perform a legal duty, whereas Prohibition stops a lower court.", "shortcut": "Prohibition = Stop lower court; Mandamus = Public duty; Quo Warranto = By what authority."},
    {"id": 14, "compendiumNo": 164, "subCategory": "FR, DPSP & Duties", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Under the Right against Exploitation (Article 24, NCERT Class IX & XI), employment of children below what age is prohibited in dangerous jobs like factories and mines?", "options": {"a": "Below 12 years", "b": "Below 14 years", "c": "Below 16 years", "d": "Below 18 years"}, "correctOption": "b", "correctedSentence": "Below 14 years", "rule": "Article 24 forbids employing children below 14 years in hazardous jobs like factories and mines; Article 23 prohibits human trafficking and begar.", "distractor": "Option (d) — 18 years is the voting age (Article 326).", "shortcut": "Article 24 (Child labour ban) & Article 21A (Free education) = Age 14 cutoff."},
    {"id": 15, "compendiumNo": 165, "subCategory": "FR, DPSP & Duties", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Under Article 22 of the Indian Constitution (NCERT Class XI Chapter 2), a person arrested and detained in custody must be produced before the nearest magistrate within how many hours (excluding travel time)?", "options": {"a": "Within 12 hours", "b": "Within 24 hours", "c": "Within 48 hours", "d": "Within 72 hours"}, "correctOption": "b", "correctedSentence": "Within 24 hours", "rule": "Under Article 22(2), an arrested person must be produced before the nearest magistrate within 24 hours (excluding travel time); preventive detention normally cannot exceed 3 months without Advisory Board review.", "distractor": "Option (c) — 48 hours is a common distractor; Article 22 strictly mandates 24 hours.", "shortcut": "Article 22 arrest safeguard = Produce before magistrate within 24 hours."},
    {"id": 16, "compendiumNo": 166, "subCategory": "FR, DPSP & Duties", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Which is a correct distinction between Fundamental Rights (Part III) and Directive Principles of State Policy (Part IV) according to NCERT Class XI Chapter 2?", "options": {"a": "Fundamental Rights are non-justiciable, while Directive Principles are legally enforceable in courts", "b": "Fundamental Rights are justiciable and restrain the government, while Directive Principles are non- justiciable guidelines for policy-making", "c": "Both Part III and Part IV are enforceable through Article 32 writs", "d": "Directive Principles were borrowed from the US Constitution"}, "correctOption": "b", "correctedSentence": "Fundamental Rights are justiciable, while Directive Principles are non-justiciable guidelines", "rule": "Part III (Fundamental Rights) is justiciable; Part IV (DPSP, borrowed from Ireland) is non- justiciable and guides state policy.", "distractor": "Option (a) — Reverses justiciable vs non-justiciable status.", "shortcut": "Fundamental Rights (Part III) = Justiciable; DPSP (Part IV) = Non-justiciable."},
    {"id": 17, "compendiumNo": 167, "subCategory": "FR, DPSP & Duties", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "By which Constitutional Amendment were Fundamental Duties (Article 51A, Part IVA) inserted into the Constitution on the recommendation of the Swaran Singh Committee (NCERT Class XI Chapter 2)?", "options": {"a": "42nd Amendment Act, 1976", "b": "44th Amendment Act, 1978", "c": "73rd Amendment Act, 1992", "d": "86th Amendment Act, 2002"}, "correctOption": "a", "correctedSentence": "42nd Amendment Act, 1976", "rule": "The 42nd Amendment (1976) inserted 10 Fundamental Duties (inspired by USSR); the 86th Amendment (2002) added the 11th Duty.", "distractor": "Option (d) — 86th Amendment added only the 11th Fundamental Duty.", "shortcut": "42nd Amendment (1976) = Added 10 Fundamental Duties; 86th Amendment = 11th Duty."},
    {"id": 18, "compendiumNo": 168, "subCategory": "FR, DPSP & Duties", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Which of the following is NOT one of the six freedoms guaranteed to all citizens under Article 19 of the Indian Constitution (NCERT Class IX & XI)?", "options": {"a": "Freedom of speech and expression", "b": "Freedom to assemble peacefully without arms", "c": "Freedom to form associations and unions", "d": "Freedom to acquire, hold, and dispose of property as a Fundamental Right"}, "correctOption": "d", "correctedSentence": "Freedom to acquire, hold, and dispose of property as a Fundamental Right", "rule": "Article 19(1)(f) (right to property) was deleted by the 44th Amendment (1978), leaving 6 freedoms under Article 19.", "distractor": "Option (b) — Peaceful assembly 'without arms' is a genuine Article 19 freedom.", "shortcut": "Article 19 now has 6 freedoms (Property deleted by 44th Amendment, 1978)."},
    {"id": 19, "compendiumNo": 169, "subCategory": "FR, DPSP & Duties", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Under Cultural and Educational Rights (Articles 29 and 30, NCERT Class XI Chapter 2), which groups have the right to establish and administer educational institutions of their choice?", "options": {"a": "Only linguistic minorities", "b": "Only religious minorities", "c": "Both religious and linguistic minorities", "d": "Only residents of Scheduled Areas"}, "correctOption": "c", "correctedSentence": "Both religious and linguistic minorities", "rule": "Article 30 grants both religious and linguistic minorities the right to establish and administer educational institutions of their choice.", "distractor": "Option (b) — Students often forget that minorities under Article 30 include both religious and linguistic groups.", "shortcut": "Article 30 Minorities = Both Religious AND Linguistic minorities."},
    {"id": 20, "compendiumNo": 170, "subCategory": "FR, DPSP & Duties", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "When was the National Human Rights Commission (NHRC) established by law in India to inquire into complaints of human rights violations (NCERT Class XI Chapter 2)?", "options": {"a": "1950", "b": "1976", "c": "1993", "d": "2005"}, "correctOption": "c", "correctedSentence": "1993", "rule": "NHRC was established in 1993; it lacks direct prosecution power and makes recommendations to the government or courts.", "distractor": "Option (d) — 2005 is the year of the Right to Information (RTI) Act.", "shortcut": "NHRC established = 1993; RTI Act = 2005. Chapter 3: Union Executive & Parliament of India — NCERT Class XI Chapters 4 & 5 (Q171–Q180)"},
    {"id": 21, "compendiumNo": 171, "subCategory": "Executive & Parliament", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "With reference to the election of the President of India (NCERT Class XI Chapter 4), who elects the President?", "options": {"a": "Direct election by all adult citizens of India", "b": "Elected Members of Parliament (LS & RS) and Elected Members of State Legislative Assemblies (MLAs)", "c": "All members (elected and nominated) of Parliament and State Legislatures", "d": "Only elected members of Lok Sabha and Legislative Councils"}, "correctOption": "b", "correctedSentence": "Elected MPs and Elected MLAs", "rule": "The President is elected indirectly by an electoral college of elected MPs and elected MLAs (including Delhi & Puducherry); nominated members and MLCs do not vote.", "distractor": "Option (c) — Nominated members are excluded from the Presidential Electoral College.", "shortcut": "President Election = ONLY Elected MPs + Elected MLAs (no nominated members, no MLCs)."},
    {"id": 22, "compendiumNo": 172, "subCategory": "Executive & Parliament", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Which constitutional amendment capped the size of the Council of Ministers (including the PM) at 15 per cent of the total members of the Lok Sabha or State Assembly (NCERT Class XI Chapter 4)?", "options": {"a": "42nd Amendment Act, 1976", "b": "52nd Amendment Act, 1985", "c": "91st Amendment Act, 2003", "d": "101st Amendment Act, 2016"}, "correctOption": "c", "correctedSentence": "91st Amendment Act, 2003", "rule": "The 91st Amendment (2003) capped the Council of Ministers at 15% of the Lok Sabha / State Assembly strength.", "distractor": "Option (b) — 52nd Amendment (1985) introduced the Anti-Defection Law (10th Schedule).", "shortcut": "15% cap on Council of Ministers = 91st Amendment Act, 2003."},
    {"id": 23, "compendiumNo": 173, "subCategory": "Executive & Parliament", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "What is the term of a member of the Rajya Sabha, and what fraction of its members retire every two years (NCERT Class XI Chapter 5)?", "options": {"a": "Term: 5 years; one-half retire every two years", "b": "Term: 6 years; one-third retire every two years", "c": "Term: 6 years; one-fourth retire every two years", "d": "Term: 4 years; one-third retire every year"}, "correctOption": "b", "correctedSentence": "Term: 6 years; one-third retire every two years", "rule": "Rajya Sabha is a permanent House; members serve 6-year terms, 1/3rd retire every 2 years, and 12 members are nominated by the President.", "distractor": "Option (a) — 5 years is the Lok Sabha's normal term.", "shortcut": "Rajya Sabha = Permanent House + 6-year term + 1/3rd retire every 2 years."},
    {"id": 24, "compendiumNo": 174, "subCategory": "Executive & Parliament", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "With reference to a 'Money Bill' (Article 110, NCERT Class XI Chapter 5), which statement is INCORRECT?", "options": {"a": "A Money Bill can be introduced only in the Lok Sabha, not in the Rajya Sabha", "b": "The Rajya Sabha can approve or suggest changes, but cannot reject it", "c": "If Rajya Sabha takes no action within 14 days, the Bill is deemed passed", "d": "A joint sitting under Article 108 can be summoned to resolve a deadlock over a Money Bill"}, "correctOption": "d", "correctedSentence": "A joint sitting under Article 108 can be summoned for a Money Bill", "rule": "There is NO joint sitting for a Money Bill or Constitutional Amendment Bill; Speaker certifies a Money Bill, and Rajya Sabha gets 14 days.", "distractor": "Option (c) — 14 days is indeed the exact time limit for the Rajya Sabha.", "shortcut": "Money Bill (Art 110) = Lok Sabha only + Speaker certifies + 14 days + NO joint sitting."},
    {"id": 25, "compendiumNo": 175, "subCategory": "Executive & Parliament", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Which special power belongs EXCLUSIVELY to the Rajya Sabha and NOT to the Lok Sabha (NCERT Class XI Chapter 5)?", "options": {"a": "Passing a No-Confidence Motion against the Council of Ministers", "b": "Authorizing Parliament to legislate on a State List matter (Article 249) or create new All-India Services (Article 312)", "c": "Introducing and passing the Annual Budget", "d": "Certifying a bill as a Money Bill"}, "correctOption": "b", "correctedSentence": "Authorizing Parliament on State List (Art 249) or All-India Services (Art 312)", "rule": "Only the Rajya Sabha can authorize Parliament to make laws on a State List subject (Art 249) or create new All-India Services (Art 312).", "distractor": "Option (a) — No-Confidence Motions can only be introduced in the Lok Sabha.", "shortcut": "Rajya Sabha exclusive powers = State List transfer (Art 249) & All-India Services (Art 312)."},
    {"id": 26, "compendiumNo": 176, "subCategory": "Executive & Parliament", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Under the Anti-Defection Law (Tenth Schedule, 52nd Amendment Act, 1985, NCERT Class XI Chapter 5), who is the final deciding authority on disqualification on grounds of defection?", "options": {"a": "The President of India", "b": "The Election Commission of India", "c": "The Presiding Officer of the House (Speaker/Chairman)", "d": "The Chief Justice of India"}, "correctOption": "c", "correctedSentence": "The Presiding Officer of the House (Speaker/Chairman)", "rule": "The Presiding Officer (Speaker in Lok Sabha/Assembly; Chairman in Rajya Sabha) decides defection disqualifications under the 10th Schedule.", "distractor": "Option (b) — Election Commission advises on office-of-profit disqualifications, not 10th Schedule defection.", "shortcut": "Anti-Defection (10th Schedule, 1985) deciding authority = Presiding Officer (Speaker/Chairman)."},
    {"id": 27, "compendiumNo": 177, "subCategory": "Executive & Parliament", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Who acts as the ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha and is elected by an electoral college consisting of all members of both Houses of Parliament (NCERT Class XI Chapter 4)?", "options": {"a": "The Prime Minister of India", "b": "The Vice-President of India", "c": "The Speaker of the Lok Sabha", "d": "The Leader of the House in Rajya Sabha"}, "correctOption": "b", "correctedSentence": "The Vice-President of India", "rule": "The Vice-President is ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha and is elected by all MPs (elected and nominated) of both Houses; MLAs do not vote.", "distractor": "Option (c) — The Speaker presides over the Lok Sabha.", "shortcut": "Vice-President = Ex-officio Rajya Sabha Chairman + Elected by ALL MPs (no MLAs)."},
    {"id": 28, "compendiumNo": 178, "subCategory": "Executive & Parliament", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Under Article 111 (NCERT Class XI Chapter 4), when the President sends an Ordinary Bill back to Parliament for reconsideration and Parliament passes it again, what must the President do?", "options": {"a": "The President can return it a second time", "b": "The President must give assent to the Bill", "c": "The Bill automatically lapses", "d": "The Bill is referred to the Supreme Court"}, "correctOption": "b", "correctedSentence": "The President must give assent to the Bill", "rule": "Upon re-passage by Parliament, the President must give assent (suspensive veto); however, having no constitutional deadline enables the 'Pocket Veto' (1986 Post Office Bill).", "distractor": "Option (a) — A suspensive veto can only be used once.", "shortcut": "Re-passed Ordinary Bill → President MUST give assent (1986 Post Office Bill = Pocket Veto)."},
    {"id": 29, "compendiumNo": 179, "subCategory": "Executive & Parliament", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Which statement about the Prime Minister's appointment (NCERT Class XI Chapter 4) is true when no single party secures a clear majority in the Lok Sabha?", "options": {"a": "The Rajya Sabha elects the Prime Minister by secret ballot", "b": "The President exercises situational discretion to appoint the leader most likely to command a majority in the Lok Sabha", "c": "The Chief Justice of India becomes acting Prime Minister", "d": "Fresh general elections must be held immediately without inviting any leader"}, "correctOption": "b", "correctedSentence": "The President exercises situational discretion to appoint the leader most likely to command a majority", "rule": "In a hung Lok Sabha, the President exercises situational discretion; a non-MP appointed as PM/Minister must get elected to Parliament within 6 months.", "distractor": "Option (a) — The Rajya Sabha never elects the Prime Minister.", "shortcut": "Hung Lok Sabha = President's situational discretion; Non-MP Minister = 6 months to become MP."},
    {"id": 30, "compendiumNo": 180, "subCategory": "Executive & Parliament", "type": "Spot the Error", "prompt": "Officers of All-India Services (IAS and IPS) are recruited by UPSC and assigned to a State cadre; under whose control do they work (NCERT Class XI Chapter 4)? (a) They work under the supervision of the State Government, though dismissal can only be taken by the Central Government | (b) They can be dismissed directly by the District Magistrate or State Governor | (c) They report exclusively to the Lok Sabha Speaker | (d) They are elected every five years by local bodies", "options": {"a": "(a) Officers of All-India Services (IAS and IPS) are recruited by UPSC and assigned to a State cadre; under whose control do they work (NCERT Class XI Chapter 4)?", "b": "(b) They work under the supervision of the State Government, though dismissal can only be taken by the Central Government |", "c": "(c) They can be dismissed directly by the District Magistrate or State Governor |", "d": "(d) They report exclusively to the Lok Sabha Speaker |"}, "correctOption": "a", "correctedSentence": "State Government supervision, while only the Central Government can dismiss them", "rule": "IAS/IPS officers serve under State supervision, while ultimate disciplinary action (dismissal) rests with the Central Government.", "distractor": "Option (b) — State governments can transfer/suspend them, but only the Centre can dismiss them.", "shortcut": "IAS/IPS = State supervision + Central dismissal authority. Chapter 4: Judiciary, PIL & Indian Federalism — NCERT Class X & XI Chapters 6 & 7 (Q181–Q190)"},
    {"id": 31, "compendiumNo": 181, "subCategory": "Judiciary & Federalism", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Disputes between the Government of India and one or more States, or between two or more States, fall under which jurisdiction of the Supreme Court (NCERT Class XI Chapter 6)?", "options": {"a": "Appellate Jurisdiction", "b": "Original Jurisdiction (Article 131)", "c": "Advisory Jurisdiction (Article 143)", "d": "Writ Jurisdiction (Article 32)"}, "correctOption": "b", "correctedSentence": "Original Jurisdiction (Article 131)", "rule": "Federal disputes between Union and States or between States go directly to the Supreme Court under Original Jurisdiction (Article 131).", "distractor": "Option (c) — Advisory Jurisdiction (Art 143) deals with questions referred by the President.", "shortcut": "Union vs State / State vs State disputes = Original Jurisdiction (Article 131)."},
    {"id": 32, "compendiumNo": 182, "subCategory": "Judiciary & Federalism", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Under Article 143 of the Indian Constitution (NCERT Class XI Chapter 6), what is the legal nature of the Supreme Court's opinion given under its Advisory Jurisdiction?", "options": {"a": "The Supreme Court is bound to give advice, and the President is bound to accept it", "b": "The Supreme Court is NOT bound to give advice, and the President is NOT bound to accept such advice", "c": "It automatically becomes an Act of Parliament", "d": "It overrides any Constitutional Amendment"}, "correctOption": "b", "correctedSentence": "Neither is the SC bound to give advice, nor is the President bound to accept it", "rule": "Under Article 143, the Supreme Court is not bound to give advice, and the President is not bound to accept it.", "distractor": "Option (a) — Students often assume Supreme Court opinions are binding on the President.", "shortcut": "Article 143 Advisory Jurisdiction = Neither SC bound to give, nor President bound to accept."},
    {"id": 33, "compendiumNo": 183, "subCategory": "Judiciary & Federalism", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "How can a Judge of the Supreme Court or a High Court be removed from office before retirement age (NCERT Class XI Chapter 6)?", "options": {"a": "By an executive order of the Prime Minister", "b": "By the President only after an address by both Houses of Parliament supported by a special majority in the same session", "c": "By a simple majority resolution of the Rajya Sabha alone", "d": "By the Bar Council of India"}, "correctOption": "b", "correctedSentence": "By the President after a special majority address by both Houses in the same session", "rule": "Judges can be removed only for 'proven misbehaviour or incapacity' via special majority in both Houses in the same session (SC retirement: 65; HC retirement: 62).", "distractor": "Option (c) — Requires BOTH Houses with a special majority, not a simple majority of one House.", "shortcut": "Judge Removal = Proven misbehaviour/incapacity + Special Majority in BOTH Houses."},
    {"id": 34, "compendiumNo": 184, "subCategory": "Judiciary & Federalism", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Public Interest Litigation (PIL) in India emerged around 1979–1980 primarily through the pioneering efforts of which two Supreme Court judges (NCERT Class XI Chapter 6)?", "options": {"a": "Justice V.R. Krishna Iyer and Justice P.N. Bhagwati", "b": "Justice H.J. Kania and Justice M. Patanjali Sastri", "c": "Justice H.R. Khanna and Justice A.N. Ray", "d": "Justice J.S. Verma and Justice Ranjan Gogoi"}, "correctOption": "a", "correctedSentence": "Justice V.R. Krishna Iyer and Justice P.N. Bhagwati", "rule": "Justice V.R. Krishna Iyer and Justice P.N. Bhagwati pioneered PIL (relaxing locus standi), highlighted by the 1979 Hussainara Khatoon case.", "distractor": "Option (b) — Justice H.J. Kania was India's first Chief Justice in 1950.", "shortcut": "PIL Pioneers (1979–80) = Justice V.R. Krishna Iyer & Justice P.N. Bhagwati."},
    {"id": 35, "compendiumNo": 185, "subCategory": "Judiciary & Federalism", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "In the Seventh Schedule (NCERT Class XI Chapter 7), subjects like Education, Forests, Trade Unions, Marriage, Adoption, and Succession are placed in which list?", "options": {"a": "Union List", "b": "State List", "c": "Concurrent List", "d": "Residuary Powers"}, "correctOption": "c", "correctedSentence": "Concurrent List", "rule": "Education, Forests, Trade Unions, Marriage, Adoption, and Succession are in the Concurrent List (42nd Amendment moved Education & Forests from State to Concurrent List).", "distractor": "Option (b) — Police, Public Health, Agriculture, and Local Government are in the State List.", "shortcut": "Concurrent List key subjects = Education, Forests, Marriage, Adoption, Trade Unions."},
    {"id": 36, "compendiumNo": 186, "subCategory": "Judiciary & Federalism", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Which Commission was appointed in 1983 to examine Centre-State relations and recommended that the appointment of Governors should be strictly non-partisan (NCERT Class XI Chapter 7)?", "options": {"a": "Sarkaria Commission", "b": "Mandal Commission", "c": "Shah Commission", "d": "Kothari Commission"}, "correctOption": "a", "correctedSentence": "Sarkaria Commission", "rule": "Sarkaria Commission (appointed 1983, report 1988) reviewed Centre-State relations, Governor appointments, and sparing use of Article 356.", "distractor": "Option (b) — Mandal Commission (1979) dealt with OBC reservations.", "shortcut": "1983 Centre-State Relations + Governor guidelines = Sarkaria Commission."},
    {"id": 37, "compendiumNo": 187, "subCategory": "Judiciary & Federalism", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Under Article 356 of the Indian Constitution (NCERT Class XI Chapter 7), President's Rule can be imposed in a State if the President is satisfied that:", "options": {"a": "A financial crisis threatens the credit of India", "b": "The government of the State cannot be carried on in accordance with the provisions of the Constitution", "c": "The State fails to win medals in national games", "d": "A Governor has not visited all districts within six months"}, "correctOption": "b", "correctedSentence": "The government of the State cannot be carried on in accordance with the Constitution", "rule": "Art 352 = National Emergency; Art 356 = President's Rule in a State (approved by Parliament within 2 months, max 3 years); Art 360 = Financial Emergency.", "distractor": "Option (a) — Financial Emergency falls under Article 360.", "shortcut": "Art 352 = National Emergency; Art 356 = President's Rule in State; Art 360 = Financial."},
    {"id": 38, "compendiumNo": 188, "subCategory": "Judiciary & Federalism", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Which of the following is a 'Holding-Together' Federation where a large country divides power between constituent States and the national government (NCERT Class X Chapter 2)?", "options": {"a": "United States of America", "b": "Switzerland", "c": "India", "d": "Australia"}, "correctOption": "c", "correctedSentence": "India", "rule": "'Coming together' = USA, Switzerland, Australia; 'Holding together' = India, Spain, Belgium.", "distractor": "Options (a) & (d) — USA and Australia are 'Coming Together' federations.", "shortcut": "Coming Together = USA, Switzerland, Australia; Holding Together = India, Spain, Belgium."},
    {"id": 39, "compendiumNo": 189, "subCategory": "Judiciary & Federalism", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "How many languages are currently included in the Eighth Schedule of the Indian Constitution as 'Scheduled Languages' (NCERT Class X Chapter 2)?", "options": {"a": "14 languages", "b": "18 languages", "c": "22 languages", "d": "28 languages"}, "correctOption": "c", "correctedSentence": "22 languages", "rule": "Originally 14 languages in 1950; subsequent amendments (21st, 71st, 92nd) brought the total to 22 languages (English is not included).", "distractor": "Option (a) — 14 was the original count in 1950.", "shortcut": "8th Schedule Languages = 22 total (14 original + 8 added; English NOT included)."},
    {"id": 40, "compendiumNo": 190, "subCategory": "Judiciary & Federalism", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Which Article grants special administrative/customary provisions to several North-Eastern states such as Nagaland (371A), Assam (371B), Manipur (371C), and Mizoram (371G) (NCERT Class XI Chapter 7)?", "options": {"a": "Article 356", "b": "Article 371", "c": "Article 324", "d": "Article 280"}, "correctOption": "b", "correctedSentence": "Article 371", "rule": "Article 371 (and 371A–371J) provides asymmetrical federal safeguards for indigenous land, customary laws, and regional development.", "distractor": "Option (d) — Article 280 deals with the Finance Commission of India.", "shortcut": "Asymmetrical special state provisions (Nagaland, Mizoram, etc.) = Article 371. Chapter 5: Panchayati Raj, Municipalities & Elections — NCERT Class XI & XII Chapters 3 & 8 (Q191–Q200)"},
    {"id": 41, "compendiumNo": 191, "subCategory": "Local Govt & Elections", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "With reference to the 73rd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1992 (NCERT Class XI Chapter 8), what is the mandatory minimum reservation of seats for women across all three tiers of Panchayats?", "options": {"a": "One-fourth (25%)", "b": "One-third (33%)", "c": "One-half (50%)", "d": "Two-thirds (66%)"}, "correctOption": "b", "correctedSentence": "One-third (33%)", "rule": "The 73rd Amendment mandates at least one-third (1/3rd) reservation for women across all panchayat positions (including chairpersons), though several states have raised it to 50% by state law.", "distractor": "Option (c) — 50% is implemented via state laws, whereas the constitutional minimum is 1/3rd.", "shortcut": "73rd Amendment Constitutional minimum for women = One-third (1/3rd)."},
    {"id": 42, "compendiumNo": 192, "subCategory": "Local Govt & Elections", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Which Schedule was added by the 73rd Amendment Act (1992) listing 29 functional subjects placed within the purview of Panchayats (NCERT Class XI Chapter 8)?", "options": {"a": "Ninth Schedule", "b": "Tenth Schedule", "c": "Eleventh Schedule", "d": "Twelfth Schedule"}, "correctOption": "c", "correctedSentence": "Eleventh Schedule", "rule": "73rd Amendment = Part IX + 11th Schedule (29 Panchayat subjects); 74th Amendment = Part IXA + 12th Schedule (18 Municipal subjects).", "distractor": "Option (d) — The Twelfth Schedule belongs to the 74th Amendment (Urban Municipalities).", "shortcut": "73rd Amend = 11th Schedule (29 subjects); 74th Amend = 12th Schedule (18 subjects)."},
    {"id": 43, "compendiumNo": 193, "subCategory": "Local Govt & Elections", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Who comprises the 'Gram Sabha' in the three-tier Panchayati Raj system (NCERT Class XI Chapter 8)?", "options": {"a": "Only the elected Ward Panchs and the Sarpanch", "b": "All adult members registered as voters in the Panchayat area", "c": "Government officials posted at the Block Development Office", "d": "Members of the Zilla Parishad and State Legislature"}, "correctOption": "b", "correctedSentence": "All adult members registered as voters in the Panchayat area", "rule": "All registered adult voters in the Panchayat area constitute the Gram Sabha; elected representatives form the Gram Panchayat.", "distractor": "Option (a) — Elected Panchs and Sarpanch form the Gram Panchayat, not the Gram Sabha.", "shortcut": "Gram Sabha = All registered voters of village; Gram Panchayat = Elected Panchs + Sarpanch."},
    {"id": 44, "compendiumNo": 194, "subCategory": "Local Govt & Elections", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Which officer is responsible for conducting elections to Panchayati Raj Institutions and Urban Local Bodies every five years (NCERT Class XI Chapter 8)?", "options": {"a": "The Chief Election Commissioner of India", "b": "The State Election Commissioner (appointed by the Governor)", "c": "The District Collector", "d": "The Union Home Secretary"}, "correctOption": "b", "correctedSentence": "The State Election Commissioner", "rule": "An independent State Election Commissioner conducts Panchayat and Municipal elections (Article 243K), while a State Finance Commission reviews local finances every 5 years.", "distractor": "Option (a) — The Election Commission of India conducts Parliament, Assembly, President, and VP elections—not local bodies.", "shortcut": "Panchayat/Municipal elections = State Election Commissioner (Article 243K)."},
    {"id": 45, "compendiumNo": 195, "subCategory": "Local Govt & Elections", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Which Act extended the provisions of the 73rd Amendment to Adivasi-dominated Scheduled Areas (Fifth Schedule areas) in 1996, empowering the Gram Sabha (NCERT Class XI Chapter 8)?", "options": {"a": "PESA Act (Provisions of the Panchayats Extension to the Scheduled Areas Act, 1996)", "b": "MGNREGA Act, 2005", "c": "Forest Conservation Act, 1980", "d": "Citizenship Amendment Act, 1955"}, "correctOption": "a", "correctedSentence": "PESA Act, 1996", "rule": "Passed in 1996, PESA extended Panchayati Raj to Fifth Schedule tribal areas while safeguarding Adivasi customs, traditions, and community forest resources.", "distractor": "Option (c) — Forest Conservation Act (1980) regulates forest land diversion.", "shortcut": "1996 Tribal Panchayat Extension = PESA Act (1996)."},
    {"id": 46, "compendiumNo": 196, "subCategory": "Local Govt & Elections", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Which Constitutional Amendment lowered the minimum voting age in India from 21 years to 18 years for Lok Sabha and State Assembly elections (NCERT Class XI Chapter 3)?", "options": {"a": "44th Amendment Act, 1978", "b": "61st Amendment Act, 1989", "c": "73rd Amendment Act, 1992", "d": "86th Amendment Act, 2002"}, "correctOption": "b", "correctedSentence": "61st Amendment Act, 1989", "rule": "The 61st Amendment Act (1989, amending Article 326) reduced the voting age from 21 to 18 years.", "distractor": "Option (a) — 44th Amendment dealt with Property Rights and Emergency safeguards.", "shortcut": "Voting age 21 → 18 = 61st Amendment Act, 1989 (Article 326)."},
    {"id": 47, "compendiumNo": 197, "subCategory": "Local Govt & Elections", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Under Article 324 (NCERT Class XI Chapter 3), how can the Chief Election Commissioner (CEC) be removed from office before the expiry of their 6-year term (or age 65)?", "options": {"a": "By the Prime Minister at any time", "b": "By the President on the basis of a resolution passed by both Houses of Parliament with a special majority", "c": "By the Union Law Minister", "d": "By the Cabinet Secretary"}, "correctOption": "b", "correctedSentence": "By the President on a special majority resolution passed by both Houses of Parliament", "rule": "The CEC can be removed only in the same manner as a Supreme Court judge (special majority in both Houses); other Election Commissioners can be removed by the President on the CEC's recommendation.", "distractor": "Option (a) — Security of tenure protects the CEC from executive removal.", "shortcut": "CEC Removal (Art 324) = Same as Supreme Court Judge (Special Majority in both Houses)."},
    {"id": 48, "compendiumNo": 198, "subCategory": "Local Govt & Elections", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "Who was the first Chief Election Commissioner of independent India, who oversaw the historic first general elections of 1951–52 (NCERT Class XII Chapter 2)?", "options": {"a": "Sukumar Sen", "b": "T.N. Seshan", "c": "K.V.K. Sundaram", "d": "S.P. Sen Verma"}, "correctOption": "a", "correctedSentence": "Sukumar Sen", "rule": "Sukumar Sen became India's first Chief Election Commissioner in January 1950 and conducted the first general elections (Oct 1951 – Feb 1952).", "distractor": "Option (b) — T.N. Seshan was a prominent CEC in the 1990s.", "shortcut": "First CEC of India (1950) = Sukumar Sen."},
    {"id": 49, "compendiumNo": 199, "subCategory": "Local Govt & Elections", "type": "Spot the Error", "prompt": "Which is the key difference between the 'First Past the Post' (FPTP) system used in India's Lok Sabha elections and the 'Proportional Representation' (PR) system (NCERT Class XI Chapter 3)? (a) In FPTP, the country is divided into single-member constituencies and the candidate who secures the highest number of votes (even without 50%+1) wins | (b) In FPTP, voters vote only for a party list | (c) In PR, a candidate must always win over 75% of votes | (d) India uses PR for Lok Sabha and FPTP for Rajya Sabha", "options": {"a": "(a) Which is the key difference between the 'First Past the Post' (FPTP) system used in India's Lok Sabha elections and the 'Proportional Representation' (PR) system (NCERT Class XI Chapter 3)?", "b": "(b) In FPTP, the country is divided into single-member constituencies and the candidate who secures the highest number of votes (even without 50%+1) wins |", "c": "(c) In FPTP, voters vote only for a party list |", "d": "(d) In PR, a candidate must always win over 75% of votes |"}, "correctOption": "a", "correctedSentence": "Single-member constituencies where the highest vote-getter wins", "rule": "India uses FPTP for Lok Sabha, State Assemblies, and Panchayats, and Proportional Representation (Single Transferable Vote) for President, VP, Rajya Sabha, and Legislative Councils.", "distractor": "Option (d) — Reverses Lok Sabha (FPTP) and Rajya Sabha (PR).", "shortcut": "FPTP = Highest vote-getter wins single seat (Lok Sabha); PR = President, VP, Rajya Sabha."},
    {"id": 50, "compendiumNo": 200, "subCategory": "Local Govt & Elections", "type": "NCERT MCQ", "prompt": "In India's Lok Sabha elections (NCERT Class XI Chapter 3), who decides which constituencies are to be reserved for Scheduled Castes (SC) and Scheduled Tribes (ST)?", "options": {"a": "The Prime Minister's Office", "b": "The Delimitation Commission (appointed by the President and working with the Election Commission)", "c": "The Supreme Court of India", "d": "The Speaker of the outgoing Lok Sabha"}, "correctOption": "b", "correctedSentence": "The Delimitation Commission", "rule": "The Delimitation Commission (appointed by the President, working with the Election Commission) draws constituency boundaries and determines SC/ST reserved seats.", "distractor": "Option (d) — The Speaker has no role in demarcating or reserving electoral constituencies.", "shortcut": "Constituency boundaries & SC/ST reservation = Delimitation Commission."}
